```

For symbol 0: `libelf_symname` returns -ESRCH. Then `ret < 0 && ret != -ESRCH` is false (ret IS -ESRCH). So we fall through to `strcmp(loadinfo->iobuffer, name)`. If `iobuffer` was never allocated (still NULL), strcmp crashes.

BUT: before this loop runs, `libelf_findsymtab` was called (in libelf_bind or in libelf_findsymbol itself). And during libelf_bind, symbols were processed. If any SHN_UNDEF symbol was encountered during libelf_bind, `libelf_symvalue` would have called `libelf_symname` which allocates iobuffer. 

But if libelf_bind fails early (because exports is NULL for an undefined symbol), it returns an error BEFORE processing all sections. At that point, iobuffer might or might not be allocated depending on which sections were processed.

Actually, let me go back to the elf_loadbinary flow:

```c
// elf_loadbinary in elf.c:
if (loadinfo.ehdr.e_type == ET_REL ...)
{
